# SOLID Principles

Every class, module, and interface in this codebase must follow SOLID. These rules are based on real violations found in this project.

---

## S — Single Responsibility Principle

A class must have **one reason to change**. If you can describe what a class does with "and", it has too many responsibilities.

### Hard Limits
- **Max ~300 lines per class file.** If a file exceeds this, it almost certainly violates SRP — decompose it.
- **Constructors must not exceed ~50 lines.** Complex setup belongs in dedicated builder/factory methods or extracted helper classes.

### Decomposition Strategy

When a screen or component grows beyond one responsibility, extract focused manager classes:

| Responsibility | Extract To | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Socket event wiring | `ScreenEventHandler` | `GameScreenEventHandler` listens and dispatches |
| FTUE pointer/tutorial logic | `FTUEController` | Owns pointer lifecycle, localStorage checks |
| Animation orchestration | `AnimationController` | Sequences GSAP tweens, handles `onComplete` chains |
| Timer management | `TimerManager` | Owns `setTimeout`/`setInterval`, handles cleanup |
| Game state tracking | `GameStateManager` | Owns state object, exposes state queries |

### Anti-Pattern — God Class (DO NOT)

```ts
// BAD: Screen does layout + socket events + FTUE + timers + partner branching
class GameScreen extends Container {
  // 35 instance fields, 1100 lines, 40+ methods
  constructor(data: StoreData) {
    super();
    // 200 lines of initialization...
  }
}
```

### Correct Pattern

```ts
// GOOD: Screen delegates to focused managers
class GameScreen extends Container {
  private eventHandler: GameScreenEventHandler;
  private ftueController: FTUEController;
  private animationController: GameAnimationController;
  private timerManager: TimerManager;

  constructor(deps: GameScreenDeps) {
    super();
    this.eventHandler = deps.eventHandler;
    this.ftueController = deps.ftueController;
    // Screen only handles layout and delegates behavior
  }
}
```

### Known Violations to Fix
- `screens/GameScreen.ts` — layout + socket events + FTUE + timers + animation + partner branching
- `screens/TournamentGameScreen.ts` — same problem, plus tournament-specific logic
- `screens/NewMatchMakingScreen.ts` — 4-phase animation state machine + socket events + timer + layout
- `components/LobbySelection.ts` — filtering + scrolling + currency logic + layout + animations
- `network/eventListeners.ts` — network event routing + PixiJS overlay rendering + game state tracking

### Rule for `network/` Layer
Files in `network/` must NEVER create or manage PixiJS display objects (`Container`, `Graphics`, `Text`, `Sprite`). The network layer handles data only. If a socket event needs to trigger UI, it must emit a typed event or call a callback — never import UI components directly.

---

## O — Open/Closed Principle

Classes should be **open for extension, closed for modification**. Adding a new variant (partner, game mode, phase) must not require editing existing shared code.

### Partner Branching Rule

**NEVER** write this:

```ts
// BAD: Every new partner modifies this file
if (CURRENT_PARTNER === PARTNER_ID.bt) {
  socketManager.disconnect();
  return;
}
if (CURRENT_PARTNER === PARTNER_ID.gs || CURRENT_PARTNER === PARTNER_ID.em) {
  navigation.goBackToLobby(true);
  return;
}
```

**ALWAYS** use the Strategy pattern:

```ts
// GOOD: New partner = new strategy class, zero changes to GameScreen
const behavior = getPartnerBehavior(CURRENT_PARTNER);
behavior.onGameEnd(context);
```

See `partner-architecture.mdc` for the full pattern.

### Game Mode Variants

When Duel and Tournament modes share behavior, extract it to a **shared base class or mixin** — never duplicate entire files.

```ts
// GOOD: Shared base, mode-specific overrides
abstract class BaseGameScreen<TPlayerBar extends IProfileBar> extends Container {
  protected abstract createPlayerBar(): TPlayerBar;
  protected abstract createOpponentBar(): IProfileBar;
  // Shared: event wiring, FTUE, animation, layout
}

class DuelGameScreen extends BaseGameScreen<PlayerProfileBar> { /* overrides only */ }
class TournamentGameScreen extends BaseGameScreen<TournamentPlayerProfileBar> { /* overrides only */ }
```

---

## L — Liskov Substitution Principle

Subclasses must be substitutable for their base class without breaking behavior.

### Inheritance Rules
- Never duplicate a class to make a variant. Extract a shared abstract base and extend it.
- Use **generics** when the only difference between two parallel classes is a type parameter (e.g., profile bar type).
- If a subclass overrides a method and changes its contract (e.g., ignores a parameter, throws where base doesn't), refactor the hierarchy.

### Known Violations
- `GameScreen` / `TournamentGameScreen` — parallel classes sharing ~70% code, no shared base
- `PhaseOne` / `TournamentPhaseOne` — identical logic, different profile bar types
- `PhaseTwo` / `TournamentPhaseTwo` — same pattern

### Fix Pattern for Phases

```ts
// GOOD: Generic base phase, concrete types only differ in profile bar
abstract class BasePhaseOne<TPlayerBar extends IProfileBar, TOpponentBar extends IProfileBar> extends Container {
  constructor(deps: PhaseOneDeps<TPlayerBar, TOpponentBar>) { ... }
  // All shared logic lives here
}

class DuelPhaseOne extends BasePhaseOne<PlayerProfileBar, OpponentProfileBar> {}
class TournamentPhaseOne extends BasePhaseOne<TournamentPlayerProfileBar, DealerProfileBar> {}
```

---

## I — Interface Segregation Principle

No class should be forced to depend on methods or properties it doesn't use.

### Rules
- **Split fat types.** If a type has optional fields that only apply to one mode, split it into focused interfaces.
- **One interface per concern.** Socket event handlers, profile bars, and state objects each get their own interface.
- **Consumers import only what they need.** A phase class should receive a `PhaseState` interface, not the entire `StoreData`.

### Known Violation — `StoreData`

```ts
// BAD: Fat type mixing duel + tournament concerns
type StoreData = {
  gameUserId: string;
  matchId: string;
  // ... 15+ properties, half are optional tournament-only fields
  currentRound?: number;
  totalRounds?: number;
  roundState?: ROUND_STATE;
  leaderboard?: Array<...>;
};
```

```ts
// GOOD: Focused interfaces
interface BaseGameState { gameUserId: string; matchId: string; gameState: GAME_STATE; }
interface DuelGameState extends BaseGameState { players: DuelPlayers; }
interface TournamentGameState extends BaseGameState { currentRound: number; totalRounds: number; leaderboard: LeaderboardEntry[]; }
```

---

## D — Dependency Inversion Principle

High-level modules must not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions.

### Rules
- **No direct singleton imports in screen/phase classes.** Pass dependencies via constructor as interfaces.
- **Define interfaces for all services:** `ISocketManager`, `INavigator`, `IApiClient`, `IAudioManager`, `ILogger`.
- **Module-level singletons are allowed** only at the composition root (entry point / factory). They must implement an interface and be injected into consumers.

### Anti-Pattern (DO NOT)

```ts
// BAD: Direct imports of concrete singletons
import { socketManager } from "../network/SocketManager";
import { navigation } from "../utils/navigation";
import { apiClient } from "../network/apis";
import { Logger } from "../utils/logger";

class GameScreen extends Container {
  constructor(data: StoreData) {
    socketManager.on(EVENTS.TURN_INFO, this.handleTurnInfo);
    // Untestable — can't mock socketManager
  }
}
```

### Correct Pattern

```ts
// GOOD: Dependencies injected as interfaces
interface GameScreenDeps {
  socket: ISocketManager;
  navigator: INavigator;
  logger: ILogger;
}

class GameScreen extends Container {
  constructor(private deps: GameScreenDeps, data: StoreData) {
    deps.socket.on(EVENTS.TURN_INFO, this.handleTurnInfo);
    // Testable — pass mock implementations
  }
}
```

### Network Layer Boundary
`SocketManager` must NEVER import UI components (`InfoPopup`, `navigation`). When disconnection occurs, `SocketManager` should emit an event or invoke a callback provided at construction time. The UI layer registers a handler that shows the popup.
